WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:02.980
Temporary filenames should not be predictable.

2
00:00:03.080 --> 00:00:11.800
A timestamp, process ID, counter, or user-provided fragment may look unique during testing, but uniqueness is not the same as safe creation.

3
00:00:11.900 --> 00:00:17.340
Other processes may observe, collide with, or pre-create names in shared locations.

4
00:00:17.620 --> 00:00:20.180
Check-then-create workflows are fragile.

5
00:00:20.280 --> 00:00:27.400
Code that checks whether a path exists and then later opens that path is making an assumption about filesystem state over time.

6
00:00:27.500 --> 00:00:31.580
Between those two operations, the meaning of the path may change.

7
00:00:31.860 --> 00:00:37.300
Safe creation should combine unpredictable naming with atomic exclusive creation.

8
00:00:37.400 --> 00:00:46.280
The program should ask the platform or a well-reviewed wrapper to create and open a new file in one operation that fails if the chosen name is already present.

9
00:00:46.560 --> 00:00:48.960
The opened handle should become the authority.

10
00:00:49.060 --> 00:00:56.180
Once the file is created safely, code should prefer operations on the handle rather than repeatedly reinterpreting the string path.

11
00:00:56.280 --> 00:01:02.280
The path can still be useful for cleanup or coordination, but it should not be the only proof of what was opened.

12
00:01:02.560 --> 00:01:09.200
Prefer platform-supported secure temporary creation APIs or project wrappers that encode the right semantics.

13
00:01:09.300 --> 00:01:18.260
C and C++ ecosystems include many older naming helpers and convenience functions, and not all of them provide the same safety properties.

14
00:01:18.540 --> 00:01:21.820
Avoid hand-rolled randomness and naming rules.

15
00:01:21.920 --> 00:01:29.600
A local naming scheme has to get unpredictability, collision handling, permissions, and directory behavior right across platforms.

16
00:01:29.700 --> 00:01:34.740
A reviewed wrapper is easier to test, document, and maintain.

17
00:01:35.020 --> 00:01:42.140
During review, look for any code that constructs a temporary path as a string and later assumes it is safe.

18
00:01:42.240 --> 00:01:49.040
That pattern deserves careful replacement or a clear explanation of how creation remains atomic and exclusive.

19
00:01:49.320 --> 00:01:53.400
Tests should verify both success and collision behavior.

20
00:01:53.500 --> 00:02:03.660
The safe path is not only that creation succeeds under normal conditions, but also that the program handles an already-existing candidate without reusing a file it did not create.
